// 34 - Calling C++ from Thunder (the native interface)
//
// Everything so far has been Thunder. Sometimes you need something Thunder
// does not have: a fast numerical routine, a device, a library someone else
// wrote. "import native" loads a shared library built from C or C++, and
// "native func" says what one of its functions looks like, so the compiler can
// check every call exactly as it checks a Thunder call.
//
// The library used here, thunder_demo, is built with the toolchain and sits
// next to thunderc and thundervm, so Run just works. Its source is in
// lib/native/thunder_demo.cpp - about a hundred lines of plain C++ - and
// the whole interface it uses is one header, include/thunder/native.h.
//
// If Run reports "cannot load the native library" (R028), the library was not
// built: build the toolchain again (./run_tests.sh or ./run.sh on Linux,
// .\run.ps1 on Windows), which builds thunder_demo with it.
//
// Try it: open the Bytecode tab and find CALL_NATIVE - one instruction, just
// like CALL_BUILTIN. Then read lib/native/README.md to write your own.

import native "thunder_demo"

// Each line below is a function in thunder_demo.cpp. Only int, float, bool,
// string and callbacks may cross the boundary; arrays and structs may not.
native func hypot(x: float, y: float): float
native func shout(text: string): string
native func checksum(text: string): int
native func each_prime(limit: int, report: func(int))
native func apply_twice(value: int, f: func(int): int): int

println("C++ computed hypot(3, 4) = ", hypot(3, 4))     // whole numbers widen
println(shout("hello from thunder"))
println("checksum of \"thunder\" = ", checksum("thunder"))

// The other direction: C++ calls a Thunder function. each_prime runs a sieve
// in C++ and calls show() once for every prime it finds.
var primes: int[] = []
func show(p: int)
    push(primes, p)

each_prime(40, show)
println("primes below 40: ", primes)
println("that is ", len(primes), " of them, adding up to ", sum(primes))

// A callback may return a value, too.
func triple(n: int): int
    return n * 3
println("apply_twice(7, triple) = ", apply_twice(7, triple))

// A native function reports a mistake the way a built-in does: the program
// stops with runtime error R030 and the message the C++ code gave. The demo's
// divide() does that for a zero divisor - uncomment the two lines to see it.
// native func divide(a: int, b: int): int
// println(divide(1, 0))
